WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.425
Welcome back to Building Azure Security.

2
00:00:02.425 --> 00:00:05.237
This is Chapter 3: DDoS protection.

3
00:00:05.237 --> 00:00:08.324
Protected isn't a property of a public IP.

4
00:00:08.324 --> 00:00:11.858
It depends on the address, the
network behind it and the plan

5
00:00:11.858 --> 00:00:15.463
that network is linked to, and
the code has to follow all three.

6
00:00:15.463 --> 00:00:19.820
We'll start from what Microsoft Learn
gives you, see what it leaves to you,

7
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.995
read how the kit follows
each address to its network,

8
00:00:22.995 --> 00:00:25.801
and walk two labs, the
evidence and the dangers.

9
00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:28.815
The chapter is a draft
awaiting the owner's review,

10
00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:33.080
and the code is illustrative,
tested against a fictional estate.

11
00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:38.142
Why does this need code?

12
00:00:38.142 --> 00:00:41.808
Because a public IP's own setting
can say it inherits protection,

13
00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:44.781
while the network it inherits
from has no plan at all.

14
00:00:44.781 --> 00:00:49.097
By the end of this chapter you can
say, for every public IP in an estate,

15
00:00:49.097 --> 00:00:52.952
whether it's under DDoS IP
Protection or Network Protection,

16
00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:56.344
whether it sends DDoS logs
to the platform workspace,

17
00:00:56.344 --> 00:00:59.968
and whether an enabled alert
fires when it's under attack.

18
00:00:59.968 --> 00:01:03.731
And you can compute the guide's DDoS
coverage metric from the same run.

19
00:01:03.731 --> 00:01:08.287
It's for platform owners who own the plan,
security monitors who answer the alert,

20
00:01:08.287 --> 00:01:11.210
and the developers who
keep the check honest.

21
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.835
Here's Microsoft's starting point.

22
00:01:16.835 --> 00:01:20.723
Every public IP gets infrastructure
protection at no cost.

23
00:01:20.723 --> 00:01:24.887
Network Protection and IP
Protection add tuning, metrics,

24
00:01:24.887 --> 00:01:29.572
alerts and reports, and neither
covers a public IP on a NAT gateway.

25
00:01:29.572 --> 00:01:33.565
Network Protection is a plan
you link to virtual networks;

26
00:01:33.565 --> 00:01:37.059
IP Protection is enabled
on one Standard public IP.

27
00:01:37.059 --> 00:01:42.095
Microsoft's Zero Trust recommendations
check that every public IP in a virtual

28
00:01:42.095 --> 00:01:46.673
network is protected, that metrics are
on, and that diagnostic logs are on.

29
00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:49.381
And Learn's simulation
tutorial alerts on the

30
00:01:49.381 --> 00:01:52.447
metric that says whether an
address is under attack.

31
00:01:52.447 --> 00:01:55.736
DDoS protection covers
layers three and four;

32
00:01:55.736 --> 00:01:59.390
layer seven needs a web
application firewall.

33
00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:04.477
What does that leave to you?

34
00:02:04.477 --> 00:02:07.877
Inherited from which network, and
does that network have a plan?

35
00:02:07.877 --> 00:02:11.796
The property doesn't say, so the
kit follows each address to the

36
00:02:11.796 --> 00:02:16.177
resource that holds it, its subnet,
its network and that network's plan.

37
00:02:16.177 --> 00:02:18.667
NAT gateway addresses can't be protected,

38
00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:22.253
so they're reported as information
and left out of coverage.

39
00:02:22.253 --> 00:02:26.059
An alert or a diagnostic setting
made one address at a time

40
00:02:26.059 --> 00:02:29.628
misses the next address, or
goes to the wrong workspace.

41
00:02:29.628 --> 00:02:33.653
An address set to Disabled isn't
protected, whatever the network.

42
00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:37.130
And a load balancer's frontend
doesn't name its backend network,

43
00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:40.490
so the kit says it can't
resolve it rather than guessing.

44
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.378
So every public IP gets
exactly one status.

45
00:02:47.378 --> 00:02:49.965
IP Protection when its mode is enabled.

46
00:02:49.965 --> 00:02:54.228
Inherited when it inherits and its
network has protection and a plan.

47
00:02:54.228 --> 00:02:58.652
Unprotected when it's disabled, or
inherits from a network with no plan.

48
00:02:58.652 --> 00:03:02.309
Unknown when it inherits and
the kit can't find the network,

49
00:03:02.309 --> 00:03:04.366
as with a load balancer frontend.

50
00:03:04.366 --> 00:03:06.378
NAT gateway when it sits on one.

51
00:03:06.378 --> 00:03:08.865
And unattached when it's
attached to nothing.

52
00:03:08.865 --> 00:03:12.719
Coverage is protected addresses
over addresses in scope,

53
00:03:12.719 --> 00:03:17.730
the guide's metric, with NAT gateway
and unattached addresses left out.

54
00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.084
For each protected address the check reads

55
00:03:24.084 --> 00:03:26.905
its diagnostic settings
and the metric alerts.

56
00:03:26.905 --> 00:03:31.292
A log counts if one of Learn's
three DDoS categories, or all logs,

57
00:03:31.292 --> 00:03:35.420
goes to the platform workspace;
Learn says the mitigation

58
00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:39.205
reports category retires
from about mid-August 2026.

59
00:03:39.205 --> 00:03:43.815
An alert counts if it's enabled, has a
criterion on the under-attack metric,

60
00:03:43.815 --> 00:03:48.117
and its scope is the address, its
resource group or its subscription.

61
00:03:48.117 --> 00:03:54.024
That gives six rules: unprotected,
unresolvable, on a NAT gateway, no logs,

62
00:03:54.024 --> 00:04:00.590
no alert, and more than one plan, since
the guide keeps one plan for the agency.

63
00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:05.390
Who's involved?

64
00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:07.769
The platform owner owns the DDoS plan,

65
00:04:07.769 --> 00:04:10.915
the networks linked to it,
and the coverage number.

66
00:04:10.915 --> 00:04:15.590
The security monitor answers the
under-attack alert and reads the DDoS logs.

67
00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:19.103
And the security officer
keeps the decision records for

68
00:04:19.103 --> 00:04:22.615
addresses no plan can protect,
like those on a NAT gateway.

69
00:04:22.615 --> 00:04:24.740
The kit's reads need only Reader.

70
00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:29.642
The simulation the guide asks for, run
only through Learn's approved partners

71
00:04:29.642 --> 00:04:34.760
against the agency's own protected addresses,
is a person's work, not the kit's.

72
00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:42.622
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with
Pester, in the kit's folder.

73
00:04:42.622 --> 00:04:47.422
Contoso's hub network is linked to
its DDoS plan; the portal spoke isn't.

74
00:04:47.422 --> 00:04:52.148
Step 1 runs the check: four of six
addresses in scope are protected.

75
00:04:52.148 --> 00:04:54.448
Step 2 reads each address.

76
00:04:54.448 --> 00:04:57.337
The firewall's, the
application gateway's and

77
00:04:57.337 --> 00:05:00.461
the VPN gateway's addresses
inherit from the hub.

78
00:05:00.461 --> 00:05:03.022
The test app's address has IP Protection.

79
00:05:03.022 --> 00:05:07.434
The jump box's address inherits from
the portal spoke, which has no plan.

80
00:05:07.434 --> 00:05:12.170
And only the application gateway's
address has both logs and an alert.

81
00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:21.179
Step 3 counts the statuses: one with
IP Protection, three inherited,

82
00:05:21.179 --> 00:05:26.533
one unprotected, one unknown, one on
a NAT gateway and one unattached.

83
00:05:26.533 --> 00:05:28.657
Step 4 reads the findings.

84
00:05:28.657 --> 00:05:32.996
The firewall's address sends its logs
to an application team's workspace,

85
00:05:32.996 --> 00:05:35.382
so the missing-logs finding still fires.

86
00:05:35.382 --> 00:05:40.482
The test app's alert exists but is disabled,
so the missing-alert finding fires too.

87
00:05:40.482 --> 00:05:44.520
The load balancer is reported
as unknown, not guessed.

88
00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.645
And the trial plan is a
second plan with no networks.

89
00:05:47.645 --> 00:05:50.830
Step 5 runs the chapter's three tests.

90
00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:58.027
Lab 2 moves to a test
subscription in a test tenant,

91
00:05:58.027 --> 00:06:02.567
never production, with at least
one public IP in a virtual network.

92
00:06:02.567 --> 00:06:05.391
A Network Protection
plan is billed per plan;

93
00:06:05.391 --> 00:06:09.817
without one the lab still runs, and
every address shows as unprotected.

94
00:06:09.817 --> 00:06:14.483
Sign in to commercial Azure, write the
settings file, and run the check live;

95
00:06:14.483 --> 00:06:19.305
six of its seven calls aren't verified
on Learn yet, so allow them and compare.

96
00:06:19.305 --> 00:06:22.063
The guide's own query
gives each address's mode,

97
00:06:22.063 --> 00:06:25.693
and the portal shows an inherited
network's DDoS protection.

98
00:06:25.693 --> 00:06:29.243
Resolve any unknown address
by hand and record it.

99
00:06:29.243 --> 00:06:33.092
Nothing changed in Azure, so
back out by deleting the output.

100
00:06:33.092 --> 00:06:35.460
The authors haven't run this lab.

101
00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:43.410
Each output supports a specific control
from the guide's requirements table.

102
00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.485
Every address's protection,
the network it depends on,

103
00:06:46.485 --> 00:06:50.538
and the coverage support NIST's
denial-of-service protection control,

104
00:06:50.538 --> 00:06:53.123
aligned to FedRAMP's
indicator that cites it.

105
00:06:53.123 --> 00:06:56.020
DDoS logs and an enabled
attack alert on each

106
00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:59.073
protected address support
system monitoring.

107
00:06:59.073 --> 00:07:03.021
And the addresses no plan can
protect, or the kit couldn't resolve,

108
00:07:03.021 --> 00:07:06.373
are named for the security
officer's decision records.

109
00:07:06.373 --> 00:07:09.284
The simulation report and
the fired alert are the

110
00:07:09.284 --> 00:07:12.197
guide's test evidence; the
kit doesn't produce them.

111
00:07:12.197 --> 00:07:16.610
The kit's output supports these
controls; the assessor decides.

112
00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:22.623
What breaks, and what guards against it?

113
00:07:22.623 --> 00:07:26.084
Trust the inherited setting
and you may count an address

114
00:07:26.084 --> 00:07:29.547
whose network has no plan, so
the kit follows it to the plan.

115
00:07:29.547 --> 00:07:32.921
Guess about a load balancer
and coverage is claimed for a

116
00:07:32.921 --> 00:07:36.223
backend nobody checked, so
it's counted as not protected.

117
00:07:36.223 --> 00:07:41.534
An alert that exists but is switched off
pages nobody, so only enabled alerts count.

118
00:07:41.534 --> 00:07:45.329
Logs to the wrong workspace leave
the monitor's queries empty,

119
00:07:45.329 --> 00:07:47.710
so only the platform workspace counts.

120
00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:52.741
And Learn warns that a protected network
can't be moved, and disabling protection

121
00:07:52.741 --> 00:07:57.380
to move it resets the tuning: that's
the change record's job, not the kit's.

122
00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:02.480
Here's what done looks like.

123
00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:07.168
Every unprotected address has a
plan, a decision record or a ticket.

124
00:08:07.168 --> 00:08:11.718
Every address the kit couldn't resolve
was resolved by hand and recorded.

125
00:08:11.718 --> 00:08:14.321
Every protected address has DDoS logs to

126
00:08:14.321 --> 00:08:17.155
the platform workspace
and an enabled alert.

127
00:08:17.155 --> 00:08:20.655
The coverage in the summary is
the number the metric reports.

128
00:08:20.655 --> 00:08:24.892
And a simulation ran this year, with
its report filed beside the run.

129
00:08:24.892 --> 00:08:29.880
Next, Chapter 4 moves to layer
seven: the web application firewall.
